---
// ═══ S-projects — five records, a status badge each, and no date anywhere ══
//
// TWO ARTEFACTS DESIGNED FROM ONE FILE: S-projects (populated) and E-projects
// (empty). Everything else is plan 12's or plan 13's and is declared `inherits`.
//
// ═══ THE STATE AXIS IS A ROUTE PARAM. `?state=` DOES NOT WORK ══════════════
// Under `output: 'static'` Astro strips the query string in DEV as well as at
// build (astro/dist/core/request.js:20 - `if (isPrerendered) url.search = "";`).
// Plan 12 proved it with seven byte-identical bodies and plan 13 made the
// rest-param route the prescriptive idiom. This plan's frontmatter names
// `src/pages/admin/projects.astro`, which would produce a screen with no state
// axis at all; the idiom wins and the deviation is recorded in the SUMMARY.
//
// ═══ THIS ROUTE AND ITS SIBLING DETAIL ROUTE DO NOT COLLIDE ════════════════
// `/admin/projects/[...state]` and `/admin/projects/[id]/[...state]` both match
// a two-segment tail. Astro's routeComparator (core/routing/priority.js:23-27)
// ranks a segment carrying a spread BELOW one that is merely dynamic, so the
// detail route is tried first; when its getStaticPaths has no entry for the id
// (`empty`, `loading`, …) the dev server falls through to this route rather
// than 404ing (core/routing/dev.js:39). At build time there is no ambiguity at
// all, because the emitted paths are disjoint. Read out of the installed astro
// before either file was written, not discovered by watching a gate fail.
//
// ═══ D-45: STATUS, NEVER A DATE ════════════════════════════════════════════
// A reader learns whether a project is real and current without the page
// ageing. So there is no "updated", no "last commit", no year, and the
// acceptance grep for `20[0-9]{2}` on this file must find nothing. The status
// values are read out of 00-COPY/one-liners.md, which is canonical for them;
// none is invented here.
//
// ═══ A LIST, NOT A GRID — AND THE COMPONENT CHOICE IS A MEASUREMENT ════════
// ADMIN-IA says S-projects proves "that five records with a status badge are a
// list, not a grid, and that D-45's three statuses are distinguishable at badge
// size". UI-SPEC offers `DataGrid` OR a `Card` list. The Card list is the
// design, and the reason is measured rather than preferred: plan 13's F-13-2
// established that DataGrid stringifies every cell and that the ONLY path for a
// Badge into a row is a column keyed exactly `status`, routed through a private
// job-application tone map {applied, interviewing, offer, rejected}. D-45's
// three values are not in that map, so all three fall through to one neutral
// tone and the second half of S-projects' claim would be unprovable inside a
// DataGrid. The evidence block below renders that collapse rather than
// asserting it.

import {
	AlertBanner,
	Badge,
	Button,
	Card,
	Chip,
	DataGrid,
	Divider,
	EmptyState,
	Eyebrow,
	Heading,
	Link,
	Skeleton,
	Text,
} from "@akhil-saxena/design-system";
import Admin from "../../../layouts/Admin.astro";
import { CANONICAL_STATES } from "../../../lib/artefacts.mjs";
import fixtures from "../../../fixtures/projects.json";

export const SCREEN = {
	id: "projects",
	route: "/admin/projects",
	entity: "projects.json (D-24, not yet extracted)",
	layouts: ["desktop"],
	proves:
		"That five project records with a status badge read as a list rather than a grid, and that D-45's three statuses are distinguishable at badge size — which they are NOT inside DataGrid, whose only Badge path is a closed job-application tone map, so the component choice is the finding rather than a preference. It also carries E-projects, whose second line is the first place a reader meets D-24's model: a project owns its case study, so adding one here adds both.",
};

// Seven paths from one file. `state: undefined` is the base path, so the
// populated state is `/admin/projects/` and dist/admin/projects/index.html
// exists. No eighth route: D-09 gives the projects LIST no refusal — the
// refusal belongs to case-study authoring, which lives on the detail route.
export function getStaticPaths() {
	return [
		{ params: { state: undefined } },
		...CANONICAL_STATES.map((s) => ({ params: { state: s } })),
	];
}

export const STATES = [
	{ state: "populated", coverage: "designed", ref: "S-projects" },
	// DESIGNED here. E-projects is one of the five per-screen empty states
	// ADMIN-IA enumerates, and this is its only host.
	{ state: "empty", coverage: "designed", ref: "E-projects" },
	// INHERITS. Plan 13 claimed T-loading-list on /admin/photos, where the list
	// is 39 rows. Five records is not a more demanding host.
	{ state: "loading", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-loading-list" },
	// INHERITS. Plan 13 claimed T-error-inline on /admin/photos and prescribed
	// that plans 14 and 15 inherit rather than re-claim it. The state still
	// renders an INSTANCE of the warning, because "saving still works" is
	// invisible without a save control on screen.
	{ state: "error", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-error-inline" },
	// INHERITS. Plan 12 designed the badge triple on the dashboard.
	{ state: "dirty", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-dirty-badge" },
	// INHERITS. D-16's per-file diff has ZERO design-system coverage (G-7) and
	// is sketched by this plan at /admin/conflict-diff/.
	{ state: "conflict", coverage: "inherits", ref: "O-conflict-diff" },
	// INHERITS. The publish outcome is one treatment sketched once.
	{ state: "success", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-success-published" },
];

// THE READ THE STATE AXIS DEPENDS ON. Replacing it with a constant is the
// negative control for check-states.mjs.
const state = Astro.params.state ?? "populated";
const fx = fixtures[state];
if (!fx) {
	throw new Error(
		`admin/projects/[...state]: no fixture for state "${state}". getStaticPaths emits ` +
			"`undefined` plus one entry per CANONICAL_STATES member, and src/fixtures/projects.json " +
			"must carry a key for each. A state declared in STATES with no fixture is a fake " +
			"artefact: the route would answer 200, render a plausible page, and be screenshotted " +
			"under a state name it is not in.",
	);
}

const noop = () => {};

// UI-SPEC's contract table, verbatim, in named constants so the exact strings
// are greppable in one place and cannot drift as the markup moves.
const EMPTY_TITLE = "No projects yet.";
const EMPTY_DESC = "A project owns its case study, so adding one here adds both.";
const EMPTY_CTA = "Add a project";
const INLINE_ERROR = "Needs a title before this can be published.";

// D-45's three statuses mapped to three Badge tones. `Archived` is UNUSED BY
// THE REAL DATA — all five projects are Live or Maintained — so the swatch row
// below renders the third tone explicitly and says it is not from the data.
// Inventing an Archived project to fill the gap would have put a false record
// in front of a reviewer; showing the tone beside the list does not.
const STATUS_TONE = { Live: "success", Maintained: "info", Archived: "neutral" };
const STATUS_MEANS = {
	Live: "deployed and still receiving work",
	Maintained: "shipped and still mine, no work in flight",
	Archived: "finished; honest about it, and better than an undated project assumed dead",
};

const isLoading = state === "loading";
const isEmpty = fx.projects.length === 0 && !isLoading;
const isInherit = ["dirty", "conflict", "success"].includes(state);

const statusCounts = {};
for (const p of fx.projects) statusCounts[p.status] = (statusCounts[p.status] ?? 0) + 1;

// F-13-2's evidence, three rows. The column is keyed exactly `status` because
// that is the only key DataGrid routes through its Badge path at all.
const gridColumns = [
	{ key: "title", label: "Project" },
	{ key: "status", label: "Status" },
];
const gridRows = ["Live", "Maintained", "Archived"].map((s, i) => ({
	id: `dg-${i}`,
	title: s === "Archived" ? "(tone not in the data)" : s === "Live" ? "Cairn" : "hued",
	status: s,
}));

const reviewStates = ["populated", ...CANONICAL_STATES];
const hrefFor = (s) => (s === "populated" ? "/admin/projects/" : `/admin/projects/${s}/`);
---

<Admin
	title={`S-projects / ${state}`}
	active="projects"
	route="/admin/projects"
	navStates={fx.nav}
>
	<div class="pj-head" data-artefact={state === "populated" ? "S-projects" : undefined}>
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">PROJECTS</Eyebrow>
		<Heading level={2} size="lg" weight="bold">{fx.headline}</Heading>
		<Text size="sm" tone="muted">{fx.sub}</Text>
	</div>

	{/* ── T-loading-list, INHERITED ─────────────────────────────────────────
	    The shell, the sidebar and Publish stay usable while the body waits.
	    Skeleton rather than a hand-rolled shape — but NOT a component's own
	    loading prop, because the Card list has none and the DataGrid this
	    screen deliberately does not use is the only thing here that does.
	    Plan 14's F-14-5 already records that absence for Sortable and
	    RichText; a Card list is the third component with the same hole. */}
	{isLoading && (
		<div class="pj-panel" data-artefact="T-loading-list">
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">INHERITS T-loading-list</Eyebrow>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				The treatment is plan 13's, claimed on the 39-row photo list. What is specific to
				projects is that each row reserves TWO heights, not one — the card fields and the
				case-study summary beneath them — so a single-line skeleton would let the page jump by
				a whole row per record when the data lands.
			</Text>
			<Skeleton shape="text" />
			<Skeleton shape="text" />
			<Skeleton shape="text" />
			<Skeleton shape="text" />
			<Skeleton shape="text" />
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ── E-projects ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	    Copy verbatim from UI-SPEC's contract table. The second sentence is
	    D-24's whole model stated in one line, and this is the first place a
	    reader meets it: adding a project here also creates the case study,
	    because a project OWNS its case study rather than linking to one.

	    Plan 13's distinguishing rule applies — an empty state must say WHY it
	    is empty. This one is empty because nothing exists, so it carries a CTA;
	    E-category-filtered on /admin/photos is empty because a filter excluded
	    everything, so it carries none. Two reasons, two designs. */}
	{isEmpty && (
		<div class="pj-panel" data-artefact="E-projects">
			<EmptyState title={EMPTY_TITLE} description={EMPTY_DESC}>
				<Button variant="primary">{EMPTY_CTA}</Button>
			</EmptyState>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				Nothing else on the screen is broken and nothing is disabled. The Work page simply
				renders one band fewer until a project exists.
			</Text>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ── THE LIST ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	    A Card per project, one column, full width. Each row carries: the
	    title, the D-45 status Badge, the platform label as a Chip, the card
	    description, the tech chips, the live link, and the case-study summary —
	    tier and real length — because D-24 pairs the two and a list that showed
	    only the card half would hide half of what the row owns.

	    NO EDITING HAPPENS HERE. ADMIN-IA is explicit: the list is an entry
	    point, editing lives on the detail route. So every row's affordance is a
	    link to /admin/projects/<id>/ and there is not one input on this page. */}
	{!isLoading && !isEmpty && (
		<section class="pj-section">
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">FIVE PROJECTS</Eyebrow>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				{fx.projects.length} records, {Object.entries(statusCounts).map(([k, v]) => `${v} ${k}`).join(", ")}.
				Status, never a date: the row says whether a project is real and current, and it does
				not age.
			</Text>

			{fx.projects.map((p) => {
				const err = fx.titleError === p.id;
				return (
					<Card surface="subtle" padding="md" radius="lg">
						<div class="pj-row">
							<div class="pj-row-head">
								<Link href={`/admin/projects/${p.id}/`}>{p.title}</Link>
								<Badge tone={STATUS_TONE[p.status]}>{p.status}</Badge>
								<Chip>{p.label.text}</Chip>
								<code class="pj-key">{p.id}</code>
							</div>

							{err && (
								<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
									<span class="pj-warn">{INLINE_ERROR}</span>
								</Text>
							)}

							<Text size="sm" tone="secondary">{p.description}</Text>

							<div class="pj-chips">
								{p.tech.map((t) => (
									<Chip>{t}</Chip>
								))}
							</div>

							<div class="pj-meta">
								<code class="pj-key">{p.caseTier} form</code>
								<code class="pj-key">{p.caseSections.length} sections</code>
								<code class="pj-key">{p.caseWords} words</code>
								<code class="pj-key">{p.badges.length} badge{p.badges.length === 1 ? "" : "s"}</code>
							</div>

							<div class="pj-actions">
								<Link href={`/admin/projects/${p.id}/`}>Edit card and case study</Link>
								<Link href={p.href}>Open the live site</Link>
							</div>
						</div>
					</Card>
				);
			})}
		</section>
	)}

	{/* ── D-45's THREE TONES, AND THE ONE THE DATA CANNOT SHOW ──────────────
	    S-projects has to prove the three statuses are distinguishable at badge
	    size. Two of the three are in the real data; `Archived` is in none of
	    the five records, so it is rendered here as a swatch and labelled as
	    such rather than pinned onto a project that is not archived. Plan 13's
	    fixture rule, applied to a value instead of a row: a sketch may show
	    what the real content cannot produce, as long as it says so where it
	    does it. */}
	{!isLoading && (
		<section class="pj-section">
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">D-45 — THE THREE STATUSES AT BADGE SIZE</Eyebrow>
			<ul class="pj-swatches">
				{Object.keys(STATUS_TONE).map((s) => (
					<li class="pj-swatch">
						<Badge tone={STATUS_TONE[s]}>{s}</Badge>
						<Text size="sm" tone="muted">{STATUS_MEANS[s]}</Text>
						<code class="pj-key">
							{statusCounts[s] ? `${statusCounts[s]} in the data` : "0 in the data"}
						</code>
					</li>
				))}
			</ul>
		</section>
	)}

	{/* ── WHY THIS IS NOT A DataGrid — RENDERED, NOT ASSERTED ───────────────
	    Plan 13's F-13-2: DataGrid stringifies every cell, and the single escape
	    hatch is a column keyed exactly `status`, routed through a PRIVATE tone
	    map covering {applied, interviewing, offer, rejected}. D-45's values are
	    not in it, so each falls through to `tone: "neutral"` carrying the raw
	    string. The grid below is three rows keyed `status` with D-45's three
	    values in it, so the collapse is visible beside the swatch row above
	    where the same three values carry three different tones.

	    Left in place rather than worked around. Recolouring the grid locally
	    would delete the evidence, which is the workaround Core Value forbids —
	    and the practical consequence is already decided: the list above is
	    Cards, and F-13-2's proposed `render?: (row) => ReactNode` is what would
	    let it be a table instead. */}
	{!isLoading && (
		<section class="pj-section">
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">EVIDENCE — THE SAME THREE VALUES INSIDE DataGrid</Eyebrow>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				One column keyed <code class="pj-key">status</code>, which is the only key DataGrid
				routes through a Badge at all. Its tone map is closed and private, so all three D-45
				values land on one tone. Compare with the swatch row above: same values, three tones.
				This is why the list is a Card list.
			</Text>
			<DataGrid columns={gridColumns} rows={gridRows} />
		</section>
	)}

	{/* ── T-error-inline: the pointer plus what a reviewer cannot infer ─────
	    D-18's LENIENT half: the message is about PUBLISHING, saving still
	    works, and it attaches to the record it is about. The treatment is plan
	    13's; what is added here is the save control, enabled, beside it.

	    AND THE COMPONENT STILL CANNOT EXPRESS THE SEVERITY. Plan 14's F-14-3:
	    FieldErrorProps is `{ message, className }` with no tone and a fixed
	    role="alert" span in --red-ink, so D-18's lenient warning and its strict
	    publish block render identically. This screen has NO Field at all — it
	    is a list, and editing lives on the detail route — so the warning here
	    is a plain span carrying the same verbatim string, and the severity
	    problem is inherited rather than restated. */}
	{state === "error" && (
		<div class="pj-panel" data-artefact="T-error-inline">
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">INHERITS T-error-inline</Eyebrow>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				What is specific to the projects list is that the warning has no field to attach to:
				this screen holds no inputs, so the message attaches to the ROW and the fix is one
				link away on the detail screen. A list that showed the warning at the top of the page
				would make a reviewer hunt five rows for which record is incomplete.
			</Text>
			<div class="pj-actions">
				<Button variant="primary">Mark as ready</Button>
				<Button variant="secondary">Preview</Button>
				<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
					Both controls are enabled. Nothing about this state is modal, and the draft saves.
				</Text>
			</div>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ── dirty, conflict and success: the pointer, not a new treatment ───── */}
	{isInherit && (
		<div
			class="pj-inherit"
			data-artefact={state === "dirty" ? "T-dirty-badge" : state === "conflict" ? "O-conflict-diff" : "T-success-published"}
		>
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">
				{state === "dirty"
					? "INHERITS T-dirty-badge"
					: state === "conflict"
						? "INHERITS O-conflict-diff"
						: "INHERITS T-success-published"}
			</Eyebrow>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				{state === "dirty"
					? "The badge is plan 12's. What is specific to projects is that ONE record carries two publishable things — the card and the case study — so the change list has to name which half moved, or an operator reads a card-copy tweak and a rewritten case study as the same edit."
					: state === "conflict"
						? "The diff is plan 12's pointer and the design system has no diff component at all (G-7). What is specific to projects is that the file is not yet extracted: until D-24's projects.json exists, a project edit and a résumé edit are the SAME file, so every project conflict is also a résumé conflict. The extraction is what makes per-file resolution meaningful here."
						: "The outcome is plan 12's. What is specific to projects is that a case study ships as JSON like everything else — there is no pipeline and no asynchronous half — so 'committed' and 'live' mean the same thing on this screen, unlike photos."}
			</Text>
			{state === "dirty" && (
				<div class="pj-badge-row">
					<Badge tone="pending" dot>Draft</Badge>
					<Text size="sm" tone="muted">Projects, in the sidebar, in the topbar count, and here.</Text>
				</div>
			)}
			{state === "conflict" && (
				<AlertBanner
					open
					tone="warning"
					title="projects.json changed after your draft started."
					description="Resolving is per file, on its own screen. Home and Photos stay pending while this one is decided."
					dismissible={false}
				/>
			)}
		</div>
	)}

	<Divider />

	{/* ── Review band. NOT part of the design. ───────────────────────────── */}
	<aside class="pj-review">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">REVIEW CHROME / NOT PART OF THE DESIGN</Eyebrow>
		<p class="pj-review-line">
			{reviewStates.map((s) => (
				<a class="pj-review-link" href={hrefFor(s)} data-on={s === state ? "true" : undefined}>{s}</a>
			))}
		</p>
		<p class="pj-review-note">
			The detail screen and its two extra artefacts are at
			<a class="pj-review-link" href="/admin/projects/cairn/">/admin/projects/cairn/</a>
			and the per-file conflict diff is at
			<a class="pj-review-link" href="/admin/conflict-diff/">/admin/conflict-diff/</a>.
		</p>
	</aside>
</Admin>

<style>
	/* App layout CSS only, per QUAL-03. Every colour is a charcoal token.
	   Scoped rather than global is safe here: this route carries NO island, so
	   every element below is in the Astro template and receives the scoping
	   attribute. Plan 13's global-style rule applies to island hosts only. */

	.pj-head,
	.pj-panel,
	.pj-inherit,
	.pj-section {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	.pj-section {
		gap: var(--space-4);
	}

	.pj-inherit {
		border-left: 2px solid var(--wire);
		padding-left: var(--space-4);
	}

	.pj-row {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	.pj-row-head,
	.pj-meta,
	.pj-actions,
	.pj-chips,
	.pj-badge-row {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	.pj-chips {
		gap: var(--space-2);
	}

	.pj-key {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: var(--text-2xs);
		color: var(--ink-3);
	}

	.pj-warn {
		color: var(--red-ink, var(--ink));
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.pj-swatches {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}
	.pj-swatch {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	/* Two columns of swatches from the near-square foldable class up — the
	   first width where a second column is honest. One column below, which is
	   REFLOW rather than hiding: all three are present at 344px. */
	@media (min-width: 673px) {
		.pj-swatches {
			grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
		}
	}

	/* == Review chrome ===================================================== */
	.pj-review {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-2);
		margin-top: var(--space-8);
		padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
		border: 1px dashed var(--wire);
		border-radius: var(--radius-md);
	}
	.pj-review-line {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--space-3);
		margin: 0;
	}
	.pj-review-note {
		margin: 0;
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-2);
		font-size: var(--text-xs);
		color: var(--ink-3);
	}
	.pj-review-link {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: var(--text-xs);
		color: var(--ink-3);
		text-decoration: none;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
	}
	.pj-review-link[data-on="true"] {
		color: var(--ink);
		border-bottom-color: var(--wire);
	}

	/* == THE 44px FLOOR, EVERY COARSE-POINTER CLASS ========================
	   Five of the six device classes in the responsive contract are coarse
	   pointer, so the floor is the common case rather than the exception, and
	   it sits on the HIT AREA rather than on the painted control.
	   density-compact.css carries the floor for the design system's own
	   controls; the rows below are mine.

	   THE BASE RULES COME FIRST AND THE FLOOR LAST, DELIBERATELY. Plan 14
	   measured a control stuck at 30px because its coarse-pointer floor was
	   written ABOVE the base rule at the same specificity (0,1,0) and source
	   order decided it. Nothing above sets a min-height on any of these
	   selectors, and this block is the last in the file. */
	@media (pointer: coarse) {
		.pj-review-link,
		.pj-row-head .ds-atom-link,
		.pj-actions .ds-atom-link,
		.pj-review-note .ds-atom-link {
			min-height: 44px;
		}
	}
</style>
